{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He was the younger brother of Melisende of Tripoli and Eschiva of Tripoli. \n\n* **Early Life and Marriage** *\nRaymond III married Hodierna of Toron in 1152. They had three children together. Their daughter Alice of Tripoli married Bohemond III King of Jerusalem in 1163. Their son Raymond IV Count of Tripoli was born in 1155. \n\nThe death of Raymond II in 1152 led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli. Raymond III was the first-born son of Raymond II but he was still a minor at the time of his father's death. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem ruled as regent until he came of age. \n\n* **Campaigns against Muslim Forces** *\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli led several campaigns against Muslim forces in the region. He fought against the Ayyubid dynasty and the Zengid dynasty. In 1164 he fought in the Battle of Harenc against the Ayyubid forces led by Nur ad-Din. He also fought in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 against the Ayyubid forces led by Saladin. \n\nIn 1187 Raymond III Count of Tripoli was captured by Saladin during the Battle of Hattin. He was taken prisoner and was eventually killed. The County of Tripoli was then conquered by the Ayyubid dynasty and became a vassal state of the Ayyubid Empire. \n\n* **Legacy** *\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli is remembered for his military campaigns against Muslim forces in the region. He fought in several battles and was a key figure in the defense of the County of Tripoli. His legacy is also remembered through his children who went on to marry into other noble families in the region.", "logprobs": 